### =========================================
### PHASE 1: PREPARATION (Seed the Database)
### =========================================

### 1. Create a cheap Valve game
POST http://localhost:8080/videogames
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "Portal",
  "price": 9.99,
  "developer": "Valve"
}

### 2. Create an expensive Valve game
POST http://localhost:8080/videogames
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "Half-Life: Alyx",
  "price": 59.99,
  "developer": "Valve"
}

### 3. Create a cheap Nintendo game
POST http://localhost:8080/videogames
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "Hollow Knight",
  "price": 14.99,
  "developer": "Team Cherry"
}

### 4. Create an expensive Nintendo game
POST http://localhost:8080/videogames
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "Zelda: Breath of the Wild",
  "price": 69.99,
  "developer": "Nintendo"
}


### =========================================
### PHASE 2: TESTING THE FILTERS
### =========================================

### Test A: The Default "Give me everything" (Should return all 4 games)
### Proves that missing parameters don't break the endpoint.
GET http://localhost:8080/videogames


### Test B: Filter by Developer Only (Should return 2 games)
### Proves the partial filter works when Price is ignored.
GET http://localhost:8080/videogames?developer=Valve


### Test C: Filter by Max Price Only (Should return 2 games: Portal and Hollow Knight)
### Proves the partial filter works when Developer is ignored.
GET http://localhost:8080/videogames?maxPrice=20.00


### Test D: Filter by BOTH Developer and Price (Should return exactly 1 game: Portal)
### Proves that both filters apply simultaneously.
GET http://localhost:8080/videogames?developer=Valve&maxPrice=15.00


### Test E: The "Empty Results" check (Should return an empty array [])
### Proves your database doesn't crash if no data matches the filters.
GET http://localhost:8080/videogames?developer=Nintendo&maxPrice=5.00

